  "articleSummary": "The article argues that hiring AI engineers should focus on the judgment and operational ownership behind a polished demo, not résumés, buzzwords, or fluent chatbot output. It recommends defining the role and first-90-days outcome, reviewing real or reconstructed artifacts, paying for a short working exercise, and evaluating evidence such as experiments, traces, agent evaluations, permissions, idempotency, failure recovery, monitoring, and cost control. The tone is practical and cautionary, using recurring “open the hood” and “demo versus real work” metaphors, with refund duplication and timeout scenarios illustrating production risks. It is aimed at founders, hiring managers, and teams recruiting engineers for AI applications, infrastructure, research, evaluation, or product roles.",
